-- title:
--   Claim 4 — lower bound on preceding $\alpha$ mass
-- statement:
--   Fix an OBMRR instance (weights $r_i\ge0$, duration $d\ge1$), constants $\beta_1\ge\beta_2$, any sequence $Q_1,Q_2,\dots$ of proposals of at most two vertices run through Algorithm 2, a round $j$, and a vertex $i\in Q_j$. Define
--
--   $$
--   \phi_j^i=\min\left\{1-p_j^i,\frac{1-q_j^i}{2}\right\}.
--   $$
--
--   With the dual variables assigned by the OPD rule of pp.23–24 ($\alpha_{i,t}=\Delta\alpha^i_t$ if $i\in Q_t$, else $0$),
--
--   $$
--   \sum_{t=\max\{j-d+1,1\}}^{j-1}\alpha_{i,t}
--   \ge (1-\beta_1)r_i\phi_j^i
--     +(1-\beta_2)r_i(1-p_j^i-\phi_j^i).
--   $$
--
--   The inequality separates the contribution made when the vertex first becomes available from the remaining matching probability in the rental window.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The probabilities are the exact finite probabilities generated by Algorithm 2 on the given proposal sequence. The sequence is arbitrary; OPD's own sequence is one instance of it.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: the source writes the one-based window with $\max\{j-d+1,1\}$ → Lean uses the zero-based, subtraction-free predicate $t<j$ and $j<t+d$; reason: the selected rounds are identical, including at the boundary.
--   - encoding: the source uses the OPD/OCR probabilities in prose → Lean expands them as exact finite probabilities of Algorithm 2; reason: this is the OCR subroutine fixed in Theorem 2 and introduces no estimated quantity.
--   - generalization: the source states the claim for the proposals chosen by OPD (Algorithm 3) → Lean states it for every list of proposals of at most two vertices (empty rounds allowed); reason: the proof on p.24 uses only the dual rule and the OCR guarantee $\Delta\mu^i_t\ge q^i_t/2$, never how the proposals were chosen, and OPD's sequence `OPDProposals I select` is a special case.
-- source:
--   Delong, Farhadi, Niazadeh, Sivan & Udwani, Online Bipartite Matching with Reusable Resources, arXiv:2110.07084v2, Claim 4, p.24; dual assignment pp.23–24

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ReusableMatching_Supplement_OPDMatching

namespace ReusableMatching.Supplement

set_option autoImplicit false

/-- **Claim 4** of Delong et al., arXiv:2110.07084v2, p.24.

For a vertex proposed in round `j`, the sum of its `α` assignments in the preceding rental window
is bounded below by the two portions determined by `φⁱ_j`.  The constants satisfy exactly the
standing condition `β₁ ≥ β₂` from p.23.

**Formalization Note.** The paper numbers rounds from 1.  With zero-based rounds, its window
`max {j-d+1,1} ≤ t ≤ j-1` is the subtraction-free filter `t < j ∧ j < t+d`.  The probabilities
`p`, `q`, and `Δμ` are exact probabilities of Algorithm 2 on the supplied proposal history.
The paper states the claim for the proposals `Q₁,…,Q_n` chosen by OPD; here `Q` is an arbitrary
list of proposals of size at most two (empty rounds allowed), which contains OPD's sequence
`OPDProposals I select` as a special case.  The proof on p.24 uses only the dual rule and the
OCR guarantee `Δμⁱ_t ≥ qⁱ_t/2`, not how `Q` was chosen. -/
theorem claim_4 {V : Type*} [Fintype V] [LinearOrder V] {n : ℕ}
    (I : ReusableMatching.OBMRRInstance V n) (β1 β2 : ℝ) (hβ : β2 ≤ β1)
    (Q : List (Proposal V)) (i : V) (j : ℕ)
    (hj : j < Q.length) (hi : i ∈ proposalAt Q j) :
    (∑ t ∈ (Finset.range j).filter (fun t => j < t + I.d),
        opdAlpha I.r I.d β1 β2 Q i t) ≥
      (1 - β1) * I.r i * opdPhi I.d Q i j +
        (1 - β2) * I.r i * (1 - availableProbability I.d Q i j - opdPhi I.d Q i j) := by sorry

end ReusableMatching.Supplement
